  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss. x is the steep direction, so with no noise any learning rate above 0.8 makes the swings in x grow.",
    "Each step moves the ball by -learning rate × (slope + noise). The noise in each slope component has standard deviation sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch size b: 1 at b = 1 and exactly 0 at b = 64.",
    "The purple arrow is this step (it points where the ball moves next). The dark arrow is the step the full data would give. Both are drawn at 5 times their true length, at one shared scale. A finished run shows no arrows, and the step length reads 0.",
    "A step is taken five times a second while playing. A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or diverges as soon as the loss goes above 5. The trail holds the start point plus one point per step, so 41 at most.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same noise. Each new run uses the next noise values. Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim, and the loss readout always shows the true value."
  ]
}